    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: (c)</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>1. Treatment of D-glucose with dilute aqueous NaOH involves the Lobry de Bruyn-van Ekenstein transformation.<br/>2. Under alkaline conditions, glucose undergoes enolization to form an enediol intermediate.<br/>3. The enediol intermediate allows interconversion between epimers and ketoses.<br/>4. Thus, D-glucose reaches equilibrium with D-mannose (its C-2 epimer) and D-fructose (the corresponding ketose).<br/>5. Therefore, the mixture contains D-glucose, D-mannose, and D-fructose.</p>",
