    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 8</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>1. An aldohexose has 4 chiral centers in its open-chain form.<br/>2. In the pyranose ring (hemiacetal), a new chiral center is formed at the anomeric carbon (C1).<br/>3. For a fixed D-configuration, C5 is fixed.<br/>4. The remaining chiral centers (C1, C2, C3, C4) can vary, but since it's a specific aldohexose in D-configuration, we only consider the $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ anomers for the 4 diastereomers of D-aldohexoses, yielding $4 \\times 2 = 8$ stereoisomers.</p>",
