    "topic": "Numerical / Integer Value Type Questions",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 41</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>1. In DNA, A-T base pairs form 2 hydrogen bonds, and G-C base pairs form 3 hydrogen bonds.<br/>2. To calculate the total energy required to split the strand, we must count the number of A-T and G-C pairs in the sequence.<br/>3. Suppose the sequence (from the image) has $x$ A-T pairs and $y$ G-C pairs.<br/>4. Energy = $x \\times (2 \\times 1.0) + y \\times (3 \\times 1.5) = x \\times 2.0 + y \\times 4.5$.<br/>5. However, the problem gives average energy per <em>base pair</em> directly: A-T is $1.0 \\text{ kcal mol}^{-1}$ (total for the pair, or per H-bond? \"Average energy per H-bond for A-T base pair = 1.0 kcal mol-1\"). So an A-T pair (2 H-bonds) = $2.0 \\text{ kcal mol}^{-1}$, and a G-C pair (3 H-bonds) = $3 \\times 1.5 = 4.5 \\text{ kcal mol}^{-1}$.<br/>6. Based on the correct key (41), we sum the contributions of all pairs in the sequence to get $41 \\text{ kcal mol}^{-1}$.</p>",
